sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    participant CA as Certificate Authority
    participant U as User Browser (Client Cert)
    participant I as Ingress/NGINX (mTLS Gate + auth_request)
    participant F as SDL Frontend (Dashboard)
    participant B as SDL Backend (BFF + Session Store)
    participant R as RBAC Middleware (OIDC Client + API)
    participant K as Keycloak (IdP)

    %% 0) One-time PKI
    U->>CA: CSR (generate keypair locally)
    CA-->>U: client.crt / client.p12
    note over U,CA: Browser installs the client certificate for mTLS

    %% 1) mTLS Handshake
    U->>I: TLS handshake (presents client cert)
    I->>I: Verify certificate against CA (CRL/OCSP)
    alt mTLS OK
        I-->>U: TLS established
    else Failed
        I-->>U: 401 mTLS required
    end

    %% 2) Initial access to Dashboard
    U->>I: GET /app
    I->>F: Proxy /app (protected)
    F-->>U: HTML/JS (SPA loads but needs auth)

    %% 3) SPA calls Backend for login
    U->>I: POST /backend/login
    I->>B: Proxy /login

    %% 4) Backend delegates login to RBAC
    B->>R: /login?rd=/app
    R-->>U: 302 → K:/authorize?client_id=rbac&redirect_uri=R:/callback

    %% 5) Browser interacts with Keycloak
    U->>K: Login (credentials/MFA)
    K-->>U: 302 → R:/callback?code=AUTH_CODE
    U->>R: GET /callback?code=AUTH_CODE

    %% 6) RBAC exchanges AUTH_CODE for tokens with Keycloak
    R->>K: POST /token (authorization_code)
    K-->>R: {access_token (JWT), id_token, refresh_token}
    R->>B: {access_token, refresh_token, claims}

    %% 7) SDL Backend creates internal session
    B->>B: Create session_id=abc123 → {tokens, claims} (Redis)
    B-->>U: 200 { session_id: "abc123" }
    note over U,B: Browser never sees the JWT, only a session_id

    %% 8) User accesses the Dashboard
    U->>I: GET /app (now authenticated)
    I->>F: GET /app
    F-->>U: Dashboard

    %% 9) Business request (e.g., transfer)
    U->>I: POST /backend/transfer\nX-Session-Id: abc123
    I->>B: /transfer
    B->>B: Resolve session_id → {access_token}
    B->>R: POST /api/transfer\nAuthorization: Bearer <access_token>
    R->>R: Validate JWT (Keycloak JWKS) & apply RBAC
    alt Authorized
        R-->>B: 200 {result}
        B-->>U: 200 {result}
    else Denied
        R-->>B: 403 Forbidden
        B-->>U: 403 Forbidden
    end

    %% 10) Token refresh (server-side)
    B->>R: POST /token (refresh_token)
    R->>K: /token (refresh_token)
    K-->>R: {new_access_token}
    R-->>B: {new_access_token}
    B->>B: Update session abc123


Highlights
	•	CA + mTLS → ensures device identity before login.
	•	RBAC Middleware is the only OIDC Client talking to Keycloak.
	•	SDL Backend never talks to Keycloak — it only delegates to RBAC.
	•	Browser never gets the JWT, it only receives a session_id.
	•	Backend injects the JWT when calling RBAC (Authorization: Bearer <access_token>).
	•	RBAC Middleware validates the JWT and applies RBAC policies.





