NGINX:

Enforces mTLS (client certificate required).

Uses auth_request to ask oauth2-proxy if the user is authenticated.

Serves Portal and proxies APIs only when oauth2-proxy says “OK”.

oauth2-proxy:

Acts as the OIDC client to Keycloak.

Builds the /authorize URL (with state/nonce), handles /callback, exchanges code→tokens.

Sets a secure session cookie (e.g. _oauth2_proxy) and injects identity headers on success.

Keeps tokens server-side (not exposed to the browser).

Keycloak: IdP (login page, MFA, token endpoint).

Portal / RBAC: receive requests only after oauth2-proxy has authenticated the session; they can consume identity headers.


✅ With this model:

Tokens never touch the browser.

Browser → NGINX requires a valid client certificate (mTLS).

RBAC Middleware is the only Keycloak client (confidential, with client_secret).




















RBAC API

The RBAC Middleware ensures privacy and enforces authorization rules across the bridge workflow. Each Issuer integrates with the RBAC Middleware to maintain an isolated privacy domain. The RBAC Middleware is responsible for authorizing mint and burn operations, ensuring only valid and approved transactions move across the networks.

⸻

4.3.4.2 Bridge interaction flow

Bridge from Enterprise to Swift SLE
	5.	A participant in the Enterprise Network initiates a deposit request via the Backend application by first approving the TokenBridge Contract to move funds from the ERC20/ERC3643 contract and locking tokens in the TokenBridge Contract.
	6.	The TokenBridge Contract emits an event with details (amount, token, destinationAddress).
	7.	The Issuer Bridge Operator monitors and captures this event via a secure message channel.
	8.	The Issuer Bridge Operator, using a gas-sponsor wallet, pays the gas on behalf of the Enterprise participant and sends the transaction details to the RBAC Middleware.
	9.	The RBAC Middleware validates the request and authorizes the mint of the private tokens inside the Swift SLE environment.

⸻

Bridge from Swift SLE to Enterprise
	6.	A participant in the Enterprise Network initiates a withdrawal request via the Backend application, which sends a request to the Issuer Bridge Operator.
	7.	The Issuer Bridge Operator forwards the withdrawal request to the RBAC Middleware for authorization.
	8.	The RBAC Middleware validates the burn, generates a proof of burn, and returns it to the Issuer Bridge Operator.
	9.	The Issuer Bridge Operator sends the validated proof back to the Enterprise TokenBridge Contract.
	10.	The TokenBridge Contract releases the locked tokens to the specified address on the Enterprise Network.

